<section xml:id="sect-addition-algorithms">
  <title>Addition Algorithms</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The addition algorithm is primarily used for numbers of 2 or more digits than can’t be easily done with mental math.
      A simple example is <m>426+172</m>.
      First, recall the expanded form of these with place-value:
      <me>
        \begin{aligned}426 + 172\amp= (400 + 20 + 6) + (100 + 70 + 2) \\\amp= (400 + 100) + (20 + 70) + (6 + 2) \\\amp= (4 + 1) \times 100 + (2+7) \times 10 + (6+2) \\\amp= 5\times 100 + 9\times 10 + 8 = 500+90+8 = 598\end{aligned}
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      Alternatively, we can see this in a column addition format:
      <me>
        \begin{aligned}\begin{array}{rr}\amp 400 \\ + \amp 100 \\ \hline \amp 500\end{array}\amp\amp\begin{array}{rr}\amp 20 \\ + \amp 70 \\ \hline \amp 90\end{array}\amp\amp\begin{array}{rr}\amp 6 \\ + \amp 2 \\ \hline \amp 8\end{array}\amp\amp\Longrightarrow\amp\amp\begin{array}{rr}\amp 426 \\ + \amp 172 \\ \hline \amp 598\end{array}\end{aligned}
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      When any of the sums within any place value is greater than 9, this operation becomes a little more difficult.
      Consider
      <me>
        \begin{aligned} 265 + 372 \amp = (200 + 60 + 5) + (300 + 70 + 2) \\ \amp = (200 + 300) + (60 + 70) + (5 + 2) \\ \amp = 500 + 130 + 7 \qquad \text{write 130 as $100 + 30$ }\\ \amp = 500 + 100 + 30 + 7 \\ \amp = 600 + 30 + 7 = 637 \end{aligned}
      </me>
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <subsection>
    <title>Prerequisite for Column Addition with Bundling</title>

    <p>
      There are a number of skills that students need to know to handle any addition problem with bundling.
      These skills include
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            the one-digit addition for all numbers <m>0</m> through 9,
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            the Place-value system of numbers from Ch.
            1.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <p>
      And more specifically, they must be able to
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            write numbers in expanded form: <m>372 = 300 + 70 + 2</m> and <m>800 + 20 + 4 = 824</m>,
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            add multiples of 10s, 100s, 1000s: <m>7+2=9</m>, <m>50+30=80</m>, <m>600 + 200=800</m>, and add a multiple of 10 to other numbers <m>53 + 30 = 83</m>,
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            compose a ten (bundling), which means taking 10 ones and making 1 ten or 10 tens and making 1 hundred.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Chip Models For Addition</title>

    <p>
      Recall that the chip model seen in Chapter <xref ref="ch-numbers" text="global" /> is that of a coin model where coins of the same denominator are placed in columns.
      Consider 325, which as a coin model would look like:
    </p>

    <image width="50%">
      <shortdescription>
      </shortdescription>
      <latex-image>
          <![CDATA[ 
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2]
            \foreach \x in {1,1.5,2} \draw (\x,1) node[draw, circle,inner sep=2pt] {100};
            \foreach \x in {1,1.5} \draw (\x,0.5) node[draw, circle,inner sep=2pt] {10};
            \foreach \x in {1,1.5,...,3} \draw (\x,0) node[draw, circle,inner sep=2pt] {1}; 
            \end{tikzpicture}
          ]]>
      </latex-image>
    </image>

    <p>
      and the location of the coins doesn’t really matter.
    </p>

    <p>
      Instead, we will put the coins in a chart as follows.
      This is 325 as a chip model.
    </p>

    <image width="80%">
      <shortdescription>
      </shortdescription>
      <latex-image>
          <![CDATA[ 
          \begin{tikzpicture}
            \draw (0,0) rectangle (6,2.5); 
            \draw (0,2)--(6,2);
            \foreach \x in {2,4} \draw (\x,0) -- (\x,2.5);
            \foreach \x/\lab in {1/100s,3/10s,5/1s} \draw (\x,2.25) node {\lab};
            \foreach \x in {1,2,3} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({0+0.3*\x},1.3) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,2} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({2+0.3*\x},1.3) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,5} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({4+0.3*\x},1.3) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \end{tikzpicture}
          ]]>
      </latex-image>
    </image>


    <paragraphs>
      <title>Chip Model with no bundling</title>

      <p>
        Let's look at an addition problem with the chip model.
        Consider the problem <m>325+213</m>, which has no bundling within any place value.
        We can develop this model with:
      </p>

      <image width="80%">
        <shortdescription>
        </shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
          <![CDATA[ 
          \begin{tikzpicture}
            \draw (0,0) rectangle (6,3); 
            \draw (0,2.5)--(6,2.5);
            \draw[dashed] (0,1.25) -- (6,1.25); 
            \foreach \x in {2,4} \draw (\x,0) -- (\x,3);
            \foreach \x/\lab in {1/100s,3/10s,5/1s} \draw (\x,2.75) node {\lab};
            \foreach \x in {1,2,3} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({0+0.3*\x},2) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,2} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({2+0.3*\x},2) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,5} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({4+0.3*\x},2) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,2}\fill[MaterialIndigo] ({0+0.3*\x},0.5) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,1}\fill[MaterialIndigo] ({2+0.3*\x},0.5) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,3}\fill[MaterialIndigo] ({4+0.3*\x},0.5) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \end{tikzpicture}
          ]]>
        </latex-image>
      </image>

      <p>
        And lastly, we will just total the chips in each column.
        The result is 5 hundreds chips, 3 tens chips and 8 ones, so 538 is the result.
        Use of the addition algorithm shows
      </p>

      <p>
        COLUMN ALGORITHM SHOWN HERE
      </p>
    </paragraphs>


    <paragraphs>
      <title>Chip Model with Bundling</title>

      <p>
        Let’s look at another chip model for addition, where the sum within a place value exceeds 9.
        Consider the example <m>27 + 46</m>.
      </p>

      <image width="60%">
        <shortdescription>
        </shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
          <![CDATA[ 
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]
            \draw (2,0) rectangle (6,3); 
            \draw (2,2.5)--(6,2.5);
            \draw[dashed] (2,1.25) -- (6,1.25); 
            \foreach \x in {4} \draw (\x,0) -- (\x,3);
            \foreach \x/\lab in {3/10s,5/1s} \draw (\x,2.75) node {\lab};
            %\foreach \x in {1,2,3} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({0+0.3*\x},2) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,2} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({2+0.3*\x},2) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,5} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({4+0.3*\x},2.2) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,2} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({4+0.3*\x},1.8) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            %\foreach \x in {1,...,2}\fill[MaterialIndigo] ({0+0.3*\x},0.5) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,4}\fill[MaterialIndigo] ({2+0.3*\x},0.5) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,5}\fill[MaterialIndigo] ({4+0.3*\x},0.7) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,1}\fill[MaterialIndigo] ({4+0.3*\x},0.3) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \end{tikzpicture}
          ]]>
        </latex-image>
      </image>

      <p>
        If we simply counted up all of the chips in a given columns there are 6 tens and 13 ones.
        This again is a case, where we need to bundle 10 ones chips and make a ten chip.
        Note: this is the identical coin model where we cashed in 10 pennies for a dime.
      </p>

      <p>
        To illustrate this on the diagram, we will draw a loop around 10 ones and produce a new ten chip.
        The ones chips that are exchanged should be crossed out.
      </p>

      <image width="60%">
        <shortdescription>
        </shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
          <![CDATA[ 
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.3]
            \draw (2,0) rectangle (6,3); 
            \draw (2,2.5)--(6,2.5);
            \draw[dashed] (2,1.25) -- (6,1.25); 
            \foreach \x in {4} \draw (\x,0) -- (\x,3);
            \foreach \x/\lab in {3/10s,5/1s} \draw (\x,2.75) node {\lab};
            %\foreach \x in {1,2,3} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({0+0.3*\x},2) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,2} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({2+0.3*\x},2) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,5} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({4+0.3*\x},2.2) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,2} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({4+0.3*\x},1.8) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            %\foreach \x in {1,...,2}\fill[MaterialIndigo] ({0+0.3*\x},0.5) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,4}\fill[MaterialIndigo] ({2+0.3*\x},0.5) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,5}\fill[MaterialIndigo] ({4+0.3*\x},0.7) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,1}\fill[MaterialIndigo] ({4+0.3*\x},0.3) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \draw[dashed, MaterialRed900, rounded corners] (4.1,0.1) -- (4.1,2.4) -- (5.8,2.4) -- (5.8,1.5) -- (4.75,1.5) -- (4.75,0.1) --  cycle; 
            \foreach \x in {1,...,5} \draw[thick] ({4+0.3*\x-0.1},2.1) -- ({4+0.3*\x+0.1},{2.3});
            \foreach \x in {1,...,2} \draw[thick] ({4+0.3*\x-0.1},1.7) -- ({4+0.3*\x+0.1},{1.9});
            \foreach \x in {1,...,2} \draw[thick] ({4+0.3*\x-0.1},0.6) -- ({4+0.3*\x+0.1},0.8);
            \foreach \x in {1,...,1} \draw[thick] ({4+0.3*\x-0.1},0.2) -- ({4+0.3*\x+0.1},0.4);
            \fill[MaterialRed900](3.5,1) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \draw[->] (4.1,1) -- (3.6,1); 
            \end{tikzpicture}
          ]]>
        </latex-image>
      </image>

      <p>
        And now the number in each column is less than 10, so this is complete.
        Now we count 7 tens chips and 3 ones chips for a total of 73.
      </p>

      <p>
        The column algorithm version of this uses numbers for chips, but the result is the same.
      </p>

      <p>
        COLUMN ADDITION FOR 27+46
      </p>

      <p>
        where the small 1 in the tens column is the bundled chip from the tens column in the model above.
      </p>

      <p>
        This procedure is often called <em>carrying</em>, however that term is a bit misleading in that it sounds like digits are moved from one column to another, which is not true, they are <em>rebundled</em>.
        Worse yet, is the term <em>borrow</em> when we get to subtraction (when is it returned?) This term will be called <em>unbundling.</em>
      </p>

      <p>
        Mastery of adding two 2-digit numbers results in more difficult problems with bundling.
        In increasing order of difficulty:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              3-digit plus 2-digit (bundle ones),
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              3-digit plus 3-digit (bundle ones),
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              3-digit plus 3-digit (bundle tens),
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              3-digit plus 3-digit (bundle both ones and tens and or hundreds).
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              4 or more digit numbers with bundling.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        We show one more example that is relatively difficult, $637+528$ using the chip model.
        First, we note that we will need a thousands column in the chart as well.
      </p>

      <image width="50%">
        <shortdescription>
        </shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
          <![CDATA[ 
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.3]
            \draw (0,0) rectangle (8,3); 
            \draw (0,2.5)--(8,2.5);
            \draw[dashed] (0,1.25) -- (8,1.25); 
            \foreach \x in {2,4,6} \draw (\x,0) -- (\x,3);
            \foreach \x/\lab in {1/1000s,3/100s,5/10s,7/1s} \draw (\x,2.75) node {\lab};
            %\foreach \x in {1,2,3} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({0+0.3*\x},2) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,3} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({2+0.3*\x},2.2) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,3} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({2+0.3*\x},1.8) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,3} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({4+0.3*\x},2) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,5} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({6+0.3*\x},2.2) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,2} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({6+0.3*\x},1.8) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            %\foreach \x in {1,...,2}\fill[MaterialIndigo] ({0+0.3*\x},0.5) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,5}\fill[MaterialIndigo] ({2+0.3*\x},0.5) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,2}\fill[MaterialIndigo] ({4+0.3*\x},0.5) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,4}\fill[MaterialIndigo] ({6+0.3*\x},0.7) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,4}\fill[MaterialIndigo] ({6+0.3*\x},0.3) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \draw[dashed, MaterialRed900, rounded corners] (6.1,0.1) rectangle (7.4,2); 
            \fill[MaterialRed900](5.5,1) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \draw[->] (6.1,1) -- (5.6,1); 
            \draw[dashed, MaterialRed900, rounded corners] (2.1,0.1) rectangle (3.35,2.5); 
            \fill[MaterialRed900](1.5,1) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \draw[->] (2.1,1) -- (1.6,1); 
            %% cross out 1s chips
            \foreach \x in {1,...,2} \draw[thick] ({6+0.3*\x-0.1},1.7) -- ({6+0.3*\x+0.1},{1.9});
            \foreach \x in {1,...,4} \draw[thick] ({6+0.3*\x-0.1},0.6) -- ({6+0.3*\x+0.1},0.8);
            \foreach \x in {1,...,4} \draw[thick] ({6+0.3*\x-0.1},0.2) -- ({6+0.3*\x+0.1},0.4);
            %% cross out 100s chips
            \foreach \x in {1,...,3} \draw[thick] ({2+0.3*\x-0.1},2.1) -- ({2+0.3*\x+0.1},2.3);
            \foreach \x in {1,...,3} \draw[thick] ({2+0.3*\x-0.1},1.7) -- ({2+0.3*\x+0.1},1.9);
            \foreach \x in {1,...,4} \draw[thick] ({2+0.3*\x-0.1},0.4) -- ({2+0.3*\x+0.1},0.6);
            \end{tikzpicture}
          ]]>
        </latex-image>
      </image>

      <p>
        When we bundle chips, you should work from the smaller to larger place values (columns), since bundling of chips in smaller denominations may affect those in larger ones.
      </p>

      <p>
        When we bundle chips, you should work from the smaller to larger place values (columns), since bundling of chips in smaller denominations may affect those in larger ones.
      </p>

      <p>
        With the bundling done, this results in 1 thousands, 1 hundred, 6 tens and 5 ones, which shows that <m>637+528=1165</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        COLUMN ADDITION FOR 637+528
      </p>

      <p>
        And note that the bundled chip in the tens column is the same as the 1 in the tens column in the diagram about.
        The bundled chip in the 1000s place is the extra 1 in the thousands column in the answer.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <remark>
      <p>
        Tips for creating good Addition Chip Models A chip model should clearly show the addition process with any bundling needed.
        Here are some features of your model that you should use.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Clearly put all addends on the diagram.
              Note you could have more than 2.
              It’s clearer if you separate each addend with a dashed line.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Show the bundling process in any of the columns that needs it.
              This includes circling and crossing out the bundled chips and using an arrow to show where those chips are bundled to.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The result should be clear.
              That is, all chips that are not crossed out should be your result.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Note: all the chip models above are good to study and to see that the tips above are included.
        Although the models in this book were produced with computer software, hand-drawn ones can work well.
        The colored chips are nice, but not required, but you should consider placing the bundled chips in locations that separate them from the addends.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Lattice Addition</title>

    <p>
      There are alternatives to performing addition in the standard way using the column algorithm.
      One of these is called <em>lattice addition</em>.
      We will show this in multiple steps with the example, <m>736+867</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <dl>
      <li>
        <title>Step 1:</title>

        <p>
          Write the addends in stacked column form with a lattice as shown below:
        </p>

        <image width="80%">
          <shortdescription>
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
          <![CDATA[ 
          \begin{tikzpicture}
            \foreach \x/\d in {4.5/7,5.5/3,6.5/6} \draw (\x,4) node {\d}; 
            \foreach \x/\d in {4.5/8,5.5/6,6.5/7} \draw (\x,3.5) node {\d}; 
            \draw (3.5,3.5) node {$+$};
            \draw (4,2) grid (7,3); 
            \foreach \x in {4,5,6} \draw[dashed] (\x,2) -- ({\x+1},3); 
            \end{tikzpicture}
          ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </li>

      <li>
        <title>Step 2:</title>

        <p>
          Add down the columns and place the ones digit below the diagonal and the tens above the diagonal.
        </p>

        <image width="80%">
          <shortdescription>
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
          <![CDATA[ 
          \begin{tikzpicture}
            \foreach \x/\d in {0/7,1/3,2/6} \draw ({\x+4.5},4) node {\d}; 
            \foreach \x/\d in {0/8,1/6,2/7} \draw ({\x+4.5},3.5) node {\d}; 
            \draw (3.5,3.5) node {$+$};
            \draw (4,2) grid (7,3); 
            \foreach \x in {4,5,6} \draw[dashed] (\x,2) -- ({\x+1},3);
            \foreach \x/\d in {0/1,1/0,2/1} \draw({\x+4.5},2.5) node [above left] {\d};
            \foreach \x/\d in {0/5,1/9,2/3} \draw({\x+4.5},2.5) node [below right] {\d}; 
            \end{tikzpicture}
          ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </li>

      <li>
        <title>Step 3:</title>

        <p>
          Extend the dashed diagonal lines to the down and right.
          Then add down the diagonal and do any bundling that is needed.
        </p>

        <p>
          For example on the second slot (with the 1 and 9 in it), the sum is 10 so write a 0, then the bundled 1 (shown below in red) in the next column.
        </p>

        <image width="80%">
          <shortdescription>
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
          <![CDATA[ 
          \begin{tikzpicture}
            \foreach \x/\d in {0/7,1/3,2/6} \draw ({\x+4.5},4) node {\d}; 
            \foreach \x/\d in {0/8,1/6,2/7} \draw ({\x+4.5},3.5) node {\d}; 
            \draw (3.5,3.5) node {$+$};
            \draw (4,2) grid (7,3); 
            \foreach \x in {3,4,5,6} \draw[dashed] ({\x-1},1) -- ({\x+1},3);
            \draw[dashed] (7,2) --(6,1); 
            \foreach \x/\d in {0/1,1/0,2/1} \draw({\x+4.5},2.5) node [above left] {\d};
            \foreach \x/\d in {0/5,1/9,2/3} \draw({\x+4.5},2.5) node [below right] {\d}; 
            \foreach \x/\d in {0/1,1/6,2/0,3/3} \draw ({\x+2.7},1) node [above] {\d};
            \draw[MaterialRed900] (4.4,1.8) node {\footnotesize 1};  
            \end{tikzpicture}
          ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </li>
      </dl>
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Base-5 Addition</title>

    <p>
      We are going to apply the addition algorithm to base-5 numbers.
      First, let’s do this using the chip or coin model.
      Let’s do <m>1023_{\text{five}}+ 2342_{\text{five}}</m>.
      First in a similar way to that of the chip model of addition in base-5, we add the chips for each number to the chart.
    </p>

    <image width="80%">
      <shortdescription>
      </shortdescription>
      <latex-image>
          <![CDATA[ 
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.3]
            \draw (0,0) rectangle (8,3); 
            \draw (0,2.5)--(8,2.5);
            \draw[dashed] (0,1.25) -- (8,1.25); 
            \foreach \x in {2,4,6} \draw (\x,0) -- (\x,3);
            \foreach \x/\lab in {1/125s,3/25s,5/5s,7/1s} \draw (\x,2.75) node {\lab};
            %\foreach \x in {1,2,3} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({0+0.3*\x},2) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,1} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({0+0.3*\x},2) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            %\foreach \x in {1,...,0} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({2+0.3*\x},2) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,2} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({4+0.3*\x},2) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,3} \fill[MaterialIndigo] ({6+0.3*\x},2) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,2}\fill[MaterialIndigo] ({0+0.3*\x},0.5) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,3}\fill[MaterialIndigo] ({2+0.3*\x},0.5) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,4}\fill[MaterialIndigo] ({4+0.3*\x},0.5) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \foreach \x in {1,...,2}\fill[MaterialIndigo] ({6+0.3*\x},0.5) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \draw[dashed, MaterialRed900, rounded corners] (6.1,0.1) rectangle (7,2.2); 
            \fill[MaterialRed900](5.5,1) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \draw[->] (6.1,1) -- (5.6,1); 
            \draw[dashed, MaterialRed900, rounded corners] (4.1,0.1) rectangle (5,2.2); 
            \fill[MaterialRed900](3.5,1) circle[radius=2.5pt];
            \draw[->] (4.1,1) -- (3.6,1); 
            %% cross out 1s chips
            \foreach \x in {1,...,3} \draw[thick] ({6+0.3*\x-0.1},1.9) -- ({6+0.3*\x+0.1},2.1);
            \foreach \x in {1,...,2} \draw[thick] ({6+0.3*\x-0.1},0.4) -- ({6+0.3*\x+0.1},0.6);
            %% cross out 5s chips
            \foreach \x in {1,...,2} \draw[thick] ({4+0.3*\x-0.1},1.9) -- ({4+0.3*\x+0.1},2.1);
            \foreach \x in {1,...,3} \draw[thick] ({4+0.3*\x-0.1},0.4) -- ({4+0.3*\x+0.1},0.6);
            %\foreach \x in {1,...,4} \draw[thick] ({2+0.3*\x-0.1},0.4) -- ({2+0.3*\x+0.1},0.6);
            \end{tikzpicture}
          ]]>
      </latex-image>
    </image>

    <p>
      Notice that when we bundle, the size of the bundle is 5 (since it is base-5).
      After the bundling step, just count.
      The result is <m>3420_{\text{five}}</m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Another thing to note is that the chip model in base-5 is the same as the chip model from above (in base-10) with the only true difference being that any number of chips more than 4 need to be bundled.
      This is precisely what we mean by base-5.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>